9lcq0iaGRuNOtpqiSUNGQ03NfTSBXhztHaf905oOmrji61TsurD99X4YXOmCmRrA4yEtQI4cX2yOj6QR-486blIGpxcUV15vnij_uXOnXdgqJ46RLSnOLAf4YRX9m3gHLCYUgHGGHbQ5WF5vuSH-oNAyvLPKZRtVHU=","summary":[]},{"id":"msg_07a2f0f9eb21f7e4016aceab8de7c487d2be853b1a1f5bc286","type":"message","status":"completed","content":[{"type":"output_text","annotations":[],"logprobs":[],"text":"Let \\(\\theta\\in[0,\\pi]\\) be the common angle and \\(t=\\cos\\theta\\). The three unit direction vectors lie in a plane, so their Gram matrix has determinant zero:\n\\[\n0=\\det\\begin{pmatrix}1&t&t\\\\t&1&t\\\\t&t&1\\end{pmatrix}\n=(1-t)^2(1+2t).\n\\]\nThus \\(t=-\\tfrac12\\) (angle \\(120^\\circ\\)) or \\(t=1\\) (all vectors point in the same direction).\n\nNow\n\\[\n|\\vec a+\\vec b+\\vec c|^2\n=1^2+2^2+3^2+2t(1\\cdot2+1\\cdot3+2\\cdot3)\n=14+22t.\n\\]\nThis gives \\(3\\) for the nonzero common angle, or \\(36\\) for the zero-angle case.\n\nFinal answer: \\(\\boxed{\\sqrt3}\\) if the common angle is nonzero; as written, the problem also permits \\(\\boxed{6}\\) when all three vectors point in the same direction."}],"phase":"final_answer","role":"assistant"}],"parallel_tool_calls":true,"presence_penalty":0,"previous_response_id":null,"prompt_cache_key":null,"prompt_cache_retention":"24h","reasoning":{"context":"all_turns","effort":"medium","mode":"standard","summary":null},"safety_identifier":null,"service_tier":"default","store":false,"temperature":1,"text":{"format":{"type":"text"},"verbosity":"medium"},"tool_choice":"auto","tool_usage":{"image_gen":{"input_tokens":0,"input_tokens_details":{"image_tokens":0,"text_tokens":0},"output_tokens":0,"output_tokens_details":{"image_tokens":0,"text_tokens":0},"total_tokens":0},"web_search":{"num_requests":0}},"tools":[],"top_logprobs":0,"top_p":0.98,"truncation":"disabled","usage":{"input_tokens":175,"input_tokens_details":{"cache_write_tokens":0,"cached_tokens":0},"output_tokens":434,"output_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":182},"total_tokens":609},"user":null,"metadat